Negative control — the SUCCESS feedback on `remove` (AC-40, ruling R10,
design D-14, parity rows F1/F2).

Deletes the `useFeedback().addSuccess(t("confirm.address_removed"))` raise from
`remove`'s settled path. The delete still fires, the cache is still invalidated,
the consumer's promise still resolves — the user is simply never told it
worked. That is the exact regression R10 protects against: both oracles raise
this feedback in the DATA layer, so a conversion that quietly relocated or lost
it would leave eight consumers silently un-notified.

Declared AC — AC-40: "removing an address, or changing my default, still tells
me it worked" — specifically its SUCCESS-DELETE quarter: `remove(id)` on
success lands exactly one SUCCESS entry carrying `confirm.address_removed`,
raised by `client-address.services.ts` itself, with NO consumer subscribed.

No collateral measured. AC-40's other three quarters are untouched by design
and stay GREEN — `remove`'s forced-422 error entry
(`error.client_address_update_failed`), and both `setDefault` halves
(`confirm.address_set_default` / `error.client_address_set_default_failed`) —
so a red here localises to the one raise that was removed rather than to
"feedback broke".

